StFstDbMaker/macro -- FST geometry (survey/alignment) tables
============================================================

What is here
------------
  fst/*.C                 the Geometry/fst survey tables, one file per
                          (table, beginTime) pair, in St_db_Maker's
                          <table>.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.C naming
  fstGeometry_db.C        reads those files and pushes them to the offline
                          MySQL DB

These four tables are chained by StFstDb::loadGeometry() to place every FST
sensor:

      fstOnTpc            FST global position inside the TPC frame   (1 row)
      hssOnFst            each of the 2 half-support structures      (2 rows)
      fstWedgeOnHss       each wedge on its HSS                      (36 rows)
      fstSensorOnWedge    each sensor on its wedge                   (108 rows)

Every entry, one line each. The dates are Gene's slots: one "ideal" and one
"survey/estimate" per run year, both before that run starts, with constants
measured from real data going any time after.

      2021-10-10 00:00:00   Run 22 ideal    identity -- misalignment off
      2021-10-15 00:00:00   Run 22 survey   the survey, all of it in hssOnFst
      2021-10-15 00:00:01   Run 22 survey   the same survey, re-decomposed
                                            <-- THIS IS WHAT WE RUN
      2023-04-10 00:00:00   Run 23 ideal    symlink to 2021-10-10
      2023-04-15 00:00:00   Run 23 survey   symlink to 2021-10-15 00:00:01
      2023-12-10 00:00:00   Run 24 ideal    symlink to 2021-10-10
      2023-12-15 00:00:00   Run 24 survey   symlink to 2021-10-15 00:00:01
      2024-12-10 00:00:00   Run 25 ideal    symlink to 2021-10-10
      2024-12-15 00:00:00   Run 25 survey   symlink to 2021-10-15 00:00:01

Symlinks, not copies, so there is one copy of every number and the later years
cannot silently drift from Run 22. If a year ever gets its own FST alignment,
replace that year's symlink with a real file.

A NEW ALIGNMENT GETS A NEW ENTRY -- never edit these. Gene's rule is to step
the time of day on the same date, so a first Run 22 alignment iteration would
go in as

      2021-10-15 00:01:00   Run 22 alignment, iteration 1

(00:00:00 and 00:00:01 on that date are taken by the two survey decompositions
above). Gene's full rule:
https://www.star.bnl.gov/protected/spin/akio/fcs/database_timestamp.txt

The DB is the truth. These files are the record of what was pushed, and the
way to override it locally.


Using these tables locally instead of the database
--------------------------------------------------
To MODIFY the geometry -- try an alignment, test a shift, reproduce an old
iteration -- do NOT edit the database. Put the tables in your working directory
and tell the chain to look only there.

  THE EASY WAY: force local-only with two bfc options.

      NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb

  NoMySQLDb drops MySQL and NoStarCintDb drops $STAR/StarDb, leaving your own
  $PWD/StarDb as the only place tables come from. Your files then win outright
  and you never have to think about timestamps. Set-up:

      mkdir -p ./StarDb/Geometry
      cp -r $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst
      # or: ln -s $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst

  then run the chain with NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb among its options. This is the
  right mode for alignment work and for any test of local table files.

  The four options, from BigFullChain.h:1159 (StBFChain.cxx:263-268 empties the
  matching directory out of the list):

      NoMySQLDb        drop MySQL
      NoStarCintDb     drop $STAR/StarDb
      NoLocalCintDb    drop $PWD/StarDb
      NoCintDb         drop BOTH $STAR/StarDb and $PWD/StarDb -> MySQL only

  Note NoStarCintDb does not clear $STAR/.$STAR_HOST_SYS/obj/StarDb, which
  stays in the list. That is harmless for us today -- the release has no
  Geometry/ tables there -- but it is not literally "nothing from $STAR".

  WITHOUT those options, ./StarDb does NOT simply win, and this is the most
  misunderstood thing here, so it is measured and quoted rather than asserted:

    * all .C files from EVERY search directory are pooled, and
      St_db_Maker::FindLeft() takes the one whose timestamp is LATEST at or
      before the requested time. Which directory it came from is irrelevant.
    * where a file and a MySQL row both exist, St_db_Maker.cxx:827 decides:
          case 3:   // Both SQL and CINT objects
            if (valsCINT[0].Get()>=valsSQL[0].Get()) { kase = 2; ... } // file
            else                                     { kase = 1; ... } // MySQL
      again the LATER beginTime wins. Files do not categorically beat the DB.
    * the list StBFChain builds is MySQL, $STAR/StarDb, $STAR/obj/StarDb,
      $PWD/StarDb, $PWD/obj/StarDb -- $PWD/StarDb is FOURTH. Position in that
      list is not precedence.

    MEASURED 2026-10-13 on FST, requesting a Run 22 date with ./StarDb holding
    the renamed tables:
        with $STAR/StarDb in the list   ->  RELEASE won  (+0.07126,-0.30102)
        with NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb     ->  OURS won     (+0.14876,-0.31702)
    The first is why the options matter: our files are stamped 20211015.000001
    and the release ships 20211110.000001, which is LATER and therefore wins, so
    edits to the local copies are silently ignored. (The resulting geometry is
    the same either way here -- the two decompositions sum to the same
    translation -- but an edit does nothing, with no warning.)

    If you cannot pass the options, the other way out is a timestamp later than
    every other entry: copy the file you want to <table>.20211113.000000.C in
    ./StarDb and edit that.

  * REMEMBER TO REMOVE IT when you want the database values back.

  * CONDOR JOBS run in their own scratch directory, so ./StarDb must exist
    THERE, not where you submitted from. Copy or link the directory into the
    job's working/tmp directory too, and pass the same options, or the job
    quietly uses something else and your test shows no change.

What each timestamp means
-------------------------
Translations are cm; no rotation has ever been non-identity. Only fstOnTpc and
hssOnFst are ever non-identity -- fstWedgeOnHss and fstSensorOnWedge are
identity in every version to date.

IMPORTANT: none of these is an alignment iteration. Every non-identity number
here is THE SURVEY. $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/fst ships it as hssOnFst at
20211110.000001:

      Left  half  t = (+0.07126, -0.30102, 0) cm
      Right half  t = (+0.22626, -0.33302, 0) cm

i.e. the FST halves sit 3.0-3.3 mm low in y and 0.7/2.3 mm off in x -- the same
order as the DB beamline y0 = -2.2 mm. FST alignment has never been iterated
past the survey.

The two Run 22 survey entries are not successive measurements and carry no new
information. 20211015.000000 is the release form, all of it in hssOnFst;
20211015.000001 lifts the common part into fstOnTpc (+0.14876, -0.31702) and
leaves the symmetric residual in hssOnFst (-/+0.07750, +/-0.01600).
fstOnTpc x hssOnFst gives the same total for both halves either way, verified
element by element. The re-decomposed form exists because the common offset
belongs in fstOnTpc physically -- the whole detector sits low, plus a small
differential between the halves -- and because an FST-global shift is then one
number to iterate instead of two. Push one of them, not both.

The "comment" field reads "Identity Matrix" in all of these files, including
the non-identity ones. It is stale, and memcpy(&row.comment,"...",1) copies a
single byte anyway, so the field carries no usable information. Do not rely on
it; this README is the record.


The "misalign" BFC option is what makes any of this take effect
---------------------------------------------------------------
The AGML/XML geometry reads these survey tables ONLY when the bfc "misalign"
option is on. Its entry in BigFullChain.h is

    {"misalign", "", "", "", "", "-AgMLideal", "Misaligned Geometry", kFALSE},

i.e. it works by taking AgMLideal OUT of the chain, which is what lets
AgPosition apply the <Misalign> entries in the geometry XML. Without it the
tables are ignored completely and the geometry is built ideal, however
carefully you edited them -- no warning, no log line, the numbers simply do not
matter.

With "misalign" on, these tables feed BOTH of:

  * fGeom.root generation. build_geom_misalign.C passes misalign=1, so the
    cached geometry is built FROM these tables. The cache is a frozen snapshot:
    once built it no longer consults the tables at all.

  * MC / simulation. The simulated detector is placed with these same
    parameters, so changing a table changes where simulated hits land, not just
    how real hits are reconstructed.

COPYING IS AS DANGEROUS AS LINKING. Both are, for different reasons, and the
copy is the one that actually catches people out:

  * a LINK means someone else's edit, or your own later edit, silently changes
    every job that resolves through it -- reconstruction, geometry building and
    MC alike -- including jobs you meant as a fixed reference.

  * a COPY gives no hint it exists. A symlink at least announces itself in
    "ls -l"; a copied directory looks like an ordinary directory and nothing
    about it says "this is shadowing the release and the database". It also
    goes stale the moment macro/ or the DB is updated, and it keeps winning
    anyway. Weeks later the numbers disagree with everyone else's and the
    reason is a directory you forgot you made.

The real hazard is not copy-versus-link, it is that ./StarDb/Geometry/* EXISTS
AT ALL and silently takes precedence. There is no log line to catch it:
St_db_Maker does not report which of the three sources a table came from, and
StFttDb only says whether it used survey tables, not where they came from
(StFstDb does not even say that much). So check by hand before you trust a
result:

      ls -ld ./StarDb/Geometry/* 2>/dev/null

which prints nothing if you are clean, "d..." if you copied, and the "-> ..."
arrow if you linked. Run it in the job's working directory too, not only where
you submitted from.

Habits that work: keep one working directory per alignment study rather than
editing in place, and delete or rename ./StarDb the moment the study is over,
instead of leaving it for next time.

And be equally careful about WHICH fGeom.root you are using. The cache and the
tables are two separate things that can disagree, and nothing checks that they
match. A stale fGeom built from older tables quietly keeps using the old
alignment while ./StarDb shows the new numbers -- which looks exactly like "the
new alignment had no effect". If you change a table, rebuild the cache, and
name it so the versions are distinguishable: that is what the sdt and label
arguments of build_geom_misalign.C are for.

Pushing to the database
-----------------------
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey22")'            dry run
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey22","writedb")'  store one slot
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("all","writedb")'       store every slot

Dry run first -- it prints every row it would write and touches nothing. Pass a
bad tag to list them all. "all" walks the eight slots in order, skipping
survey22flat, which is redundant with survey22.

Tags: ideal22 survey22flat survey22 ideal23 survey23 ideal24 survey24
      ideal25 survey25

An optional third argument restricts the push to ONE of the four tables:

      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","fstOnTpc")'

That exists only for the first push, to test write permission on the smallest
possible change. A partial update leaves the geometry inconsistent at that
timestamp -- StFstDb::loadGeometry() chains all four tables -- so finish the
other three straight away.

Writing needs INSERT permission on Geometry_fst, granted per account -- ask
Dmitry Arkhipkin <arkhipkin@bnl.gov>. Reads work without it, so a clean dry run
says nothing about whether you can push.

NEVER STORE THE SAME (table, beginTime) TWICE. The primary key is
(nodeID, elementID, beginTime, flavor, deactive), so a second store at the same
beginTime collides with the first. A new entry gets a new time of day, as in
"What is here" above.


PUSHING IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. St_db_Maker resolves ./StarDb, then
$STAR/StarDb, then MySQL, and $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/fst still holds the
release's four files at 20211110.000001. Those beat anything in the database.
Until they are removed from the STAR repository, production keeps reading the
release copy and never sees these entries. That removal is a separate PR and
the last step of this migration -- the push is not done until it has landed.

After the push and that removal:
  - rebuild any fGeom.*.root whose sdt no longer resolves to the entry you
    intend. The cache is a frozen snapshot and nothing checks it against the
    tables (see "The misalign option" above);
  - these files stay here as the record of what was pushed, and as what to copy
    into ./StarDb when you want to override it.
